description:
Implement the remaining STRICT_PORTABLE scalar domain already required by fgdb-w1-foundation-types-tjk.

Scope:
- canonical fixed-scale Decimal with explicit half-even rounding and reject-on-overflow semantics;
- timestamp values storing UTC nanoseconds plus exact offset and optional zone identifier bound to a content-addressed tzdb ObjectId;
- text values bound to content-addressed Unicode, normalization, segmentation, and non-binary collation artifacts;
- one CanonicalScalar union with coherent equality, hashing, total ordering, and canonical encode/decode; unavailable artifacts reject rather than consulting host locale or timezone data.

Constraints: std plus closed-universe workspace crates only; forbid unsafe; length-before-allocation; no host locale or clock access; no plan Markdown changes.

Acceptance:
- decimal normalization, rounding boundaries, overflow rejection, and order/encoding coherence tests;
- timestamp offset/zone validation and missing tzdb artifact rejection tests;
- binary and artifact-pinned collation validation with missing/mismatched artifact rejection;
- randomized equality/hash/order/round-trip properties include every new scalar arm;
- foundation_types_e2e transcript covers successful values and typed rejection paths and remains byte-identical across repeated runs;
- fmt, check all targets, clippy -D warnings, cargo test, and UBS pass.

## What & scope
Implement Appendix B's semantic vocabulary: intent references and coordinates, `CapturedIntentObservation`, `GraphIntent`/`GraphIntentBatch`, the closed intent operation set, canonical committed effects with provenance envelopes, `NetEffectNormalForm`, `CommittedEffectSet`, and derivation of the sequence-neutral `LogicalDeltaTemplate`. Intents are deterministic commands captured at statement execution — not committed effects and not Ripple deltas.

## Why (bets, market, goals)
B6/B5: the intent log is the replayable semantic record that makes rebase (FG-INV-17), merge, audit, and agent-facing provenance possible — every mutation has a deterministic vocabulary with captured observations, so re-evaluation under a moved head is well-defined and drift is detectable to the byte. B1: branch merge and time travel operate on this vocabulary, not on physical bytes.

## Design — normative detail
`IntentElementRef` is the closed union `Existing{database_security_namespace_id, graph, vid_or_eid} | EscapedAllocation{binding_ref:StrongRef<EscapingAllocationReservationRecord>, slot_key, permanent_value} | StatementLocal{tmp_ref_ordinal}`. `StatementLocal` can appear only within one `GraphIntentBatch` and cannot enter result bytes, a later statement, procedure/UDF output, provenance/audit, a durable witness, or a committed effect. First escape uses §4.5's ordered reservation: before an element identity or generated key can occur in buffered/public result bytes, a procedure response, an audit/provenance record, or a later statement's workspace, the owning Local or Meta stream orders an `EscapingAllocationReservationSpec` and permanently spends its exact slot under a live `IdRangeLease`; all subsequent intents use the `EscapedAllocation` arm; finalization preserves every binding byte-for-byte even after rebase, or rejects; a create that becomes a no-op or an aborted transaction leaves an authenticated spent tombstone. Only identities proved not to have escaped may be assigned during finalization from commit-order allocation. A sequence-neutral first-creation effect stores `BirthOrdinal {intent_ordinal, merge_ordinal, element_id}`; its creating `MarkerRef` supplies `commit_seq`, yielding durable `OriginBirthOrder {commit_seq, intent_ordinal, merge_ordinal, element_id}` — the only basis for `InsertionOrder` tie-breaks. `IntentCoordinateRef` is exactly `{binding_role_id, coordinate:CoordinateSchemaBinding, write_basis:WeakMarkerIdentity}`; no raw graph/branch name, run locator, or untyped reference is legal.

`CapturedIntentObservation` is the generated closed union of exact value/CAS, element-version, key absence/range gap, adjacency/degree, valid-time range, constraint, escrow, and procedure-output observations; every arm names its coordinate role, schema/contract epoch, witness ref, disclosure class, and deterministic recomputation rule. `GraphIntent<Role>` is `{authority_bound_header:AuthorityBoundHeader<Role>, intent_ordinal, intent_semantics_oid, read_coordinate_roles[], write_coordinate_refs[], captured_observation_refs[], structural_effect_class, operation:GraphIntentOperation}`. `GraphIntentBatch<Role>` is the independently addressable per-statement object `{authority_bound_header, registration_identity, statement_seq, canonical_statement_classification_ref:StrongRef<CanonicalStatementClassification>, intent_semantics_ref:StrongRef<IntentSemantics>, prior_workspace_generation_identity, sorted_intents, escaped_allocation_binding_root_ref:StrongRef<TxnAllocationBindingRoot>, statement_observation_commitment, resource_bounds, body_digest}`. Ordinals are contiguous; arrays/maps have registered size/depth/string/value limits; the batch's binding/semantics/coordinate values must match every child; a child intent cannot carry another root, service epoch, purpose, principal, or ceiling even when policy/revocation scalars match. Registration owns no future batch. `LocalStatementPublicationSpec`/`GlobalStatementPublicationSpec` must name the already-built exact batch, prior/successor workspace and cumulative roots, and the Published successor plus successor workspace/cumulative root strongly own the batch; a statement abort proves no batch exists or retains one exact failed diagnostic batch under its registered retention policy. Every shard workspace delta/certificate binds its routed batch projection, exact Shard security projection, and complete batch digest. Prepare proves a gap-free bijection between published statements, batches, workspace effects, observations, and cumulative roots.

Intent families (closed): `CreateVertex{new_ref, label_set, props, valid_time_assignment}`, `DeleteVertex{target, expected_version, cascade_policy}`, `AddLabel`/`RemoveLabel{vid, label}`, `AddEdge{new_ref, src, etype, dst, requested_key?, props, valid_time_assignment}`, `RemoveEdge{target, expected_version}`, `SetProp{elem, name, value, merge_policy_id}`, `RemoveProp{elem, name}`, `EnsureEdge{src, etype, dst, constraint_id, props}`, `CompareAndSet{elem, name, expected, value, mismatch:NoOp|StatementError|TxnAbort}`, `SetInsert{elem, name, element, set_epoch}`, `SetRemove{elem, name, element, observed_tags_or_remove_policy}`, `SetValidTime{elem, contract_id, expected:Any|Exact(ValidTimeAssignment), replacement, mismatch:NoOp|StatementError|TxnAbort}`, `AdjustCounter{elem, property, delta, algebra:ExactAdd|Escrow{constraint_id, domain_epoch}, operation_key, overflow:Reject}`, `SketchUpdate{elem_or_relation, sketch_profile_oid, operation_key, canonical_input, exact_delete_token?}`, `AssertSameAs{a, b, confidence, source}` (canonical sugar for `AddEdge(SAME_AS)` — an ordinary protected edge with stable EId; there is no second entity-resolution mechanism), `BulkLoad{logical_batch_ref:StrongRef<CanonicalBulkLogicalBatch>, source_digest, row_count, optional_staged_run_refs[ConditionalCoordinateRef]}`, `SchemaOp{canonical_schema_operation}`. `CanonicalBulkLogicalBatch` carries bounded canonical logical rows with explicit operation tag, coordinate role, element/key/value/valid-time/provenance fields, row ordinal, source-row digest, batch digest; staged runs are optional accelerators checked against the batch — no effect is inferred from physical run bytes. Catalog aliases and all branchable schema/constraint/index definitions use ordinary `SchemaOp`; database-global topology, security policy, keys, configuration, index-build progress, and derived-generation activation remain typed control commands, never `SchemaOp`. The merge-algebra semantics of the vocabulary — `EffectOrder` LWW, `ExactAdd`, escrow custody, sketch profiles, the binder-rewrite rule for governed state, and `ConsistencyClass` derivation — are specified in the split-out subtask `w5-intent-merge-algebras`.

`StatementObservationCommitment` covers the exact canonical published result bytes or NoResult proof, escaped identities/generated values, error class, workspace successor, and every read/constraint/rights observation visible to later statements. Rebase may regenerate an unpublished autocommit terminal result but may not alter any prior published commitment; if re-evaluation would change one byte, identity, row order, error/no-op choice, valid period, or workspace-visible effect, the transaction fails with a serialization/retry outcome. Finalization evaluates all commands and emits only canonical effects; the closed effect vocabulary, `EffectProvenance`, the coordinate/global envelopes, `NetEffectNormalForm`, `CommittedEffectSet`, and `LogicalDeltaTemplate` derivation are specified and implemented in the split-out subtask `w5-effects-normal-form`.

## Invariants & claim types
FG-INV-11 (restated): any value is durably reserved no later than its first escape to a result, later workspace, procedure/UDF, provenance/audit, or terminal preparation; rebase preserves prior bindings and may append only; disappearance/conflict/abort creates permanent spent tombstones. FG-INV-17: rebase executes under capability-stripped deterministic contexts pinned to `IntentSemanticsOid`; incremental rung-1 reuse is legal iff extensionally equal to full re-evaluation. FG-INV-18: canonical committed effects equal logical before/after state; workspace finalization equals ordered published statements. FG-INV-07: pre-order inputs exclude their future command/result/root (registration owns no future batch). All invariant-class; the differential template test is an oracle artifact.

## Interactions
Consumes `w5-binder`'s classification (batch names it), `w2-id-allocator` (leases, reservations, tombstones), `g0-identity-registries` (kind rows, BodyDigest transcripts). `w5-effects-normal-form` consumes the batches this bead defines and produces the canonical effects. Produces batches/effects consumed by `w4-coordinator`, `w4-merge-ladder` (rung re-evaluation), `w2-commit-protocol` (capsules), `w2-delta-batches` (`LogicalDeltaTemplate`→`LogicalDeltaBatch`), `w6-delta-consumption`, `w9-audit-pipeline` (provenance), `verif-reference`.

## Acceptance criteria & tests
Appendix B's fuzz/model campaign (shared with `w5-effects-normal-form`, which owns the normal-form/effect/delta comparisons): delete or duplicate intents/observations, reorder bulk rows, force no-op/CAS drift, replay operation keys, expose IDs then rebase/abort — compare batch application, workspace state, and reference finalization byte-for-byte. DAG and substitution tests reject a registration-to-future-batch edge, missing/extra batch, authority substitution, or reused certificate from another batch. Property tests: StatementLocal confinement (any escape path — result bytes, later statement, UDF output, provenance, witness — rejects); ordinal contiguity and registered size/depth limits enforced. E2E: multi-statement transaction script logging per-statement batch digest, observation commitment, and escaped-allocation bindings.

## Risks & rejected alternatives
Rejected: physical write-ahead records as the semantic record (not rebaseable); a second entity-resolution mutation mechanism beside `AssertSameAs`; inferring bulk effects from staged run bytes; wall-clock LWW. Risk: observation-capture completeness — an arm missing a deterministic recomputation rule breaks rebase; guarded by the generated-closed-union discipline.

## Provenance
Plan Appendix B (2655–2733), §4.5 (219–229), §8.1 classification linkage.

## Fresh-eyes exact normative attachment — Appendix B — exact Graph Intent Log vocabulary
The following audited source-plan appendix is embedded verbatim. It is authoritative for every row, arm, caveat, claim boundary, and checker obligation; the preceding elaboration supplies implementation rationale and ownership but cannot abbreviate or contradict this inventory.

## Appendix B — Graph Intent Log (the semantic vocabulary)

Intents are deterministic commands captured at statement execution; they are **not** committed effects or Ripple deltas. `IntentElementRef` is the closed union `Existing{database_security_namespace_id,graph,vid_or_eid}|EscapedAllocation{binding_ref:StrongRef<EscapingAllocationReservationRecord>,slot_key,permanent_value}|StatementLocal{tmp_ref_ordinal}`. `StatementLocal` can appear only within one `GraphIntentBatch` and cannot enter result bytes, a later statement, procedure/UDF output, provenance/audit, a durable witness, or a committed effect. First escape uses §4.5's ordered reservation and all subsequent intents use the EscapedAllocation arm. `IntentCoordinateRef` is exactly `{binding_role_id,coordinate:CoordinateSchemaBinding,write_basis:WeakMarkerIdentity}`; no raw graph/branch name, run locator, or untyped reference is legal.

`CapturedIntentObservation` is the generated closed union of exact value/CAS, element-version, key absence/range gap, adjacency/degree, valid-time range, constraint, escrow, and procedure-output observations. Every arm names its coordinate role, schema/contract epoch, witness ref, disclosure class, and deterministic recomputation rule. `GraphIntent<Role>` is `{authority_bound_header:AuthorityBoundHeader<Role>,intent_ordinal,intent_semantics_oid,read_coordinate_roles[],write_coordinate_refs[],captured_observation_refs[CapturedIntentObservationRef],structural_effect_class,operation:GraphIntentOperation}`. `GraphIntentBatch<Role>` is the independently addressable per-statement object `{authority_bound_header:AuthorityBoundHeader<Role>,registration_identity,statement_seq,canonical_statement_classification_ref:StrongRef<CanonicalStatementClassification>,intent_semantics_ref:StrongRef<IntentSemantics>,prior_workspace_generation_identity,sorted_intents,escaped_allocation_binding_root_ref:StrongRef<TxnAllocationBindingRoot>,statement_observation_commitment,resource_bounds,body_digest:BodyDigest<GraphIntentBatch<Role>>}`. Ordinals are contiguous, arrays and maps have registered size/depth/string/value limits, and the batch's binding/semantics/coordinate values must match every child. A child intent cannot carry another root, service epoch, purpose, principal, or ceiling even when policy/revocation scalars happen to match. Registration owns no future batch. `LocalStatementPublicationSpec`/`GlobalStatementPublicationSpec` must name the already-built exact batch, prior/successor workspace and cumulative roots, and the Published successor plus successor workspace/cumulative root strongly own it. A statement abort proves no batch exists or retains one exact failed diagnostic batch under its registered retention policy. Every shard workspace delta/certificate binds its routed batch projection, exact Shard security projection, and complete batch digest. Prepare proves a gap-free bijection between published statements, batches, workspace effects, observations, and cumulative roots; DAG and substitution tests reject a registration-to-future-batch edge, missing/extra batch, authority substitution, or reused certificate from another batch.

```
CreateVertex   { new_ref, label_set, props, valid_time_assignment }
DeleteVertex   { target, expected_version, cascade_policy }
AddLabel/RemoveLabel { vid, label }
AddEdge        { new_ref, src, etype, dst, requested_key?, props, valid_time_assignment }
RemoveEdge     { target, expected_version }
SetProp        { elem, name, value, merge_policy_id }
RemoveProp     { elem, name }
EnsureEdge     { src, etype, dst, constraint_id, props }
CompareAndSet  { elem, name, expected, value, mismatch: NoOp|StatementError|TxnAbort }
SetInsert      { elem, name, element, set_epoch }
SetRemove      { elem, name, element, observed_tags_or_remove_policy }
SetValidTime   { elem, contract_id, expected:Any|Exact(ValidTimeAssignment),
                 replacement:ValidTimeAssignment,
                 mismatch:NoOp|StatementError|TxnAbort }
AdjustCounter  { elem, property, delta,
                 algebra:ExactAdd|Escrow{constraint_id,domain_epoch},
                 operation_key, overflow:Reject }
SketchUpdate   { elem_or_relation, sketch_profile_oid, operation_key,
                 canonical_input, exact_delete_token? }
AssertSameAs   { a, b, confidence, source } // canonical sugar for AddEdge(SAME_AS)
BulkLoad       { logical_batch_ref:StrongRef<CanonicalBulkLogicalBatch>,
                 source_digest, row_count,
                 optional_staged_run_refs[ConditionalCoordinateRef] }
SchemaOp       { canonical_schema_operation } // ordinary transactional intent/effect
```

`CanonicalBulkLogicalBatch` contains bounded canonical logical rows with explicit operation tag, coordinate role, element/key/value/valid-time/provenance fields, row ordinal, source-row digest, and batch digest. Staged runs are optional accelerators checked against that batch; no effect is inferred from physical run bytes. `AssertSameAs` creates an ordinary protected `SAME_AS` edge with stable EId, endpoints, properties, valid time, and provenance; there is no second entity-resolution mutation mechanism. Catalog graph/branch aliases and all branchable schema/constraint/index definitions use ordinary `SchemaOp` transaction effects and the same marker/head/authorization/SSI path. Database-global topology, security policy, key, configuration, physical index-build progress, and derived-generation activation remain typed control commands, not `SchemaOp`.

LWW policies use canonical `EffectOrder { commit_seq, intent_ordinal, merge_ordinal, effect_ordinal }`, never element birth or an ambient wall clock. `ExactAdd` is checked integer/fixed-decimal addition identified by a registered algebra profile; raw addition is not idempotent, so ACI merge operates on the set-union of unique operation keys followed by deterministic checked summation. Escrow uses §4.7's custody ledger. Every sketch profile fixes its merge/delete algebra, precision/error contract, canonical state, duplicate-operation behavior, and fallback; a sketch without exact deletion either rejects delete/update workloads or rebuilds, and never becomes authoritative constraint state. Generic SetProp/RemoveProp against counter/escrow/sketch-governed state is invalid unless the binder rewrites it into the registered typed operation with captured-before/CAS semantics. `ConsistencyClass` is derived only after composing intent semantics, captured observations, active constraints, rights/authority, and branch policy. Commutativity tables are caches of proved contextual results.

`StatementObservationCommitment` covers the exact canonical published result bytes or NoResult proof, escaped identities/generated values, error class, workspace successor, and every read/constraint/rights observation visible to later statements. Rebase may freely regenerate an unpublished autocommit terminal result, but it may not alter any prior published statement commitment. If re-evaluation would change one byte, identity, row order, error/no-op choice, valid period, or workspace-visible effect, the explicit transaction fails with a serialization/retry outcome rather than silently committing a history the client did not observe.

Finalization evaluates all commands and emits only canonical effects:

```text
CreateVertexEffect { permanent_vid, allocation_binding_or_finalization_proof,
                     birth_ordinal, labels, canonical_props, valid_time_assignment }
DeleteVertexEffect { vid, complete_before_version, retirement_basis,
                     sorted_retired_incident_edge_before_images[],
                     sorted_constraint/temporal/escrow_contribution_retractions[] }
LabelMembershipEffect { vid, label, before, after }
CreateEdgeEffect { permanent_eid, allocation_binding_or_finalization_proof,
                   birth_ordinal, src, type, dst, canonical_key?, props,
                   valid_time_assignment }
DeleteEdgeEffect { eid, complete_before_version, retirement_basis,
                   sorted_constraint/temporal/escrow_contribution_retractions[] }
PropertyEffect { elem, property, before, after }
ValidTimeEffect { elem, contract_id, before, after, before_version_identity,
                  temporal_constraint_transition_refs[], witness_basis }
CounterEffect { operation_key, elem, property, algebra_profile,
                delta, before, after, overflow:Reject }
EscrowAdjustmentEffect { domain_id, epoch, operation_key, contribution_key?,
                         elem_property_or_cardinality_subject, delta,
                         before_value, after_value, consumed_reservation_refs[],
                         produced_right_kind_and_amount, target_owner,
                         conservation_basis_digest }
SketchEffect { operation_key, sketch_profile_oid, before_state_digest,
               after_state_ref, realized_answer_contract }
SchemaEffect { transition:CoordinateSchemaTransition,
               canonical_catalog_schema_constraint_index_operations[] }
ConstraintStateEffect { apply_basis, before_schema_root, before_schema_epoch,
                        after_schema_root, after_schema_epoch,
                        before_constraint_root, after_constraint_root,
                        exact_removed_and_added_contributions[] }
ResourceChargeEffect { reservation_id, scope, before_charge, after_charge, reason }
```

Every effect carries `EffectProvenance {source_batch_ref:StrongRef<GraphIntentBatch>,source_intent_ordinal,source_statement_seq,source_coordinate_role,source_observation_commitment,merge_or_import_origin?,authorization_decision_digest,canonicalization_profile_oid}`. `CoordinateEffectEnvelope {coordinate:WriteCoordinateBinding,effect_ordinal,effect,provenance}` is the sole coordinate source, so nested effects cannot disagree. `GlobalEffectEnvelope` is the closed union `Resource{transition_ref,effect}|Audit{ticket_and_event_commitment}|AuthorityGlobalEscrow{domain_id,operation_summary_ref,effect}`; a coordinate effect cannot smuggle a global charge and a global effect cannot mutate graph state. Resource reservation/charge/release ownership and audit ticket/event ownership are typed strong refs, never bare IDs or an English “handled elsewhere” promise.

`NetEffectNormalForm` groups by coordinate and logical identity/key, cancels only inverse effects proved unobserved and algebraically cancellable, preserves create/delete before-images and all unique operation keys, sorts cascades/contributions canonically, and rejects two incompatible terminal values. It includes a proof mapping every source intent to its surviving effects or an explicit `NoOp{captured_basis,policy}`; no intent disappears implicitly. `CommittedEffectSet<Role:AuthorityOwningRole>` is `{authority_bound_header:AuthorityBoundHeader<Role>,intent_semantics_ref,ordered_source_batch_refs[],statement_observation_commitments[],net_effect_normal_form_ref,coordinate_effect_envelopes[],global_effect_envelopes[],complete_allocation_binding_commitment,constraint_temporal_escrow_evidence,authorization_decision_ref:StrongRef<AuthorizationDecisionRecord<Role>>,resource_transition_refs[StrongRef<ResourceLedgerTransition<Role>>],audit_ownership_commitment,body_digest}`. It has explicit before/after semantics, permanent IDs/BirthOrdinals, branch-aware retirement bases, full cascade before-images, and no conditional operation. Every child effect/provenance/resource reference must agree with the same complete authority binding; equal policy/revocation scalars under a different principal, purpose, ceiling, or service epoch are noninterchangeable.

The sequence-neutral graph/branch/relation/schema/intent-semantics-keyed `LogicalDeltaTemplate` is derived from that normal form and differential-tested against applying the gap-free ordered batches once under the pinned `IntentSemanticsOid` to the recorded basis. Create/delete/property/valid-time/counter/escrow/sketch/schema/constraint deltas are exact typed rows; only the committed `MarkerRef` turns the template into an ordered `LogicalDeltaBatch`. Fuzz/model tests delete or duplicate intents/effects/provenance/observations, reorder bulk rows/cascades, force no-op/CAS drift, replay operation keys, expose IDs then rebase/abort, and compare batch application, workspace state, reference finalization, normal form, committed effects, and integrated deltas byte-for-byte.

### Attachment closure requirements
A checksum fixture pins this exact appendix range. Generation must enumerate every row/arm with stable IDs and map it bijectively to the owning implementation, unit/property/fuzz/simulation/E2E tests, detailed redacted diagnostic-event schemas, and consuming gate. Missing, duplicate, wildcard, silently weakened, or unowned rows fail CI and gate review.
